**Answer: No.** The set of real numbers cannot be expressed as a countable union of sets each of cardinality strictly less than $|\mathbb{R}|$.

## Proof

Let $\mathfrak{c} := |\mathbb{R}| = 2^{\aleph_0}$. We prove by contradiction, using **König's theorem**.

### König's Theorem (cardinal version)

> If $\{\kappa_i\}_{i \in I}$ and $\{\lambda_i\}_{i \in I}$ are families of cardinals with $\kappa_i < \lambda_i$ for every $i \in I$, then
> $$\sum_{i \in I} \kappa_i \;<\; \prod_{i \in I} \lambda_i.$$

### Deriving the contradiction

Suppose, for contradiction, that
$$\mathbb{R} = \bigcup_{n=0}^{\infty} A_n, \qquad |A_n| < \mathfrak{c} \text{ for every } n \in \omega.$$

Set $\kappa_n := |A_n|$ and $\lambda_n := \mathfrak{c} = 2^{\aleph_0}$ for each $n$. By hypothesis $\kappa_n < \lambda_n$ for all $n$, so König's theorem (with index set $I = \omega$) yields
$$\sum_{n \in \omega} \kappa_n \;<\; \prod_{n \in \omega} \lambda_n = \prod_{n \in \omega} 2^{\aleph_0}.$$

Now compute the right-hand side using standard cardinal arithmetic:
$$\prod_{n \in \omega} 2^{\aleph_0} = \left(2^{\aleph_0}\right)^{\aleph_0} = 2^{\,\aleph_0 \cdot \aleph_0} = 2^{\aleph_0} = \mathfrak{c},$$
where we used $\aleph_0 \cdot \aleph_0 = \aleph_0$.

Therefore
$$\sum_{n \in \omega} |A_n| \;<\; \mathfrak{c}.$$

But the union is bounded by the sum of the cardinalities:
$$\left|\bigcup_{n \in \omega} A_n\right| \;\leq\; \sum_{n \in \omega} |A_n| \;<\; \mathfrak{c} = |\mathbb{R}|.$$

This contradicts the assumption $\bigcup_{n \in \omega} A_n = \mathbb{R}$, which requires $\left|\bigcup_{n} A_n\right| = |\mathbb{R}| = \mathfrak{c}$.

### Conclusion

The contradiction shows that no such decomposition exists. Equivalently, the cofinality of the continuum satisfies
$$\operatorname{cf}(2^{\aleph_0}) > \aleph_0,$$
which is precisely the instance of König's theorem proved above. Hence $\mathbb{R}$ is **not** a countable union of sets of cardinality less than $\mathfrak{c}$. $\blacksquare$

